cartridge "JEWEL GRAB/GAZETTE/1989"

' JEWEL GRAB
' A ColecoVision game based on "Jewel Grab", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' January 1989 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2063; no author named on
' the disk). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026,
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a miner gathers every gem of a cavern screen while round bombs
' float after him through the rock. The ledges are stone blocks and the
' ladders are loose: the miner can pick up to four of each and put them
' down elsewhere to build his own way. A golden goblet is worth 200 and
' stops the bombs for a while. Six screens make a round; each round adds a
' bomb (1 to 4); clear the fourth round to win.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Stick: walk left/right, climb ladders up
' and down. Button: jump (four rows up, carried on in the direction the
' stick held). Stick down + button: pick up the block diagonally below in
' front of the miner, or put one down there. Stick up (only up) + button:
' pick up the ladder piece at the miner's legs, or put one there. On the
' title, up and down choose, left and right set the speed, a button chooses.
'
' From the original (orig/jgrab.dis; run in jsc64, which shows the play
' screen but not sprite collisions, so the hit rules come from the code):
' the screens' blocks, ladders, gems, bomb speeds and goblet places ($1276
' table, orig/levels.py); the miner moves one cell a pass: walk if the cell
' at his legs ahead is empty, a ladder or a gem ($0AD1); jump = four passes
' up while the cell above his head is empty or a gem ($0A62), the stick held
' at take-off carries on through the jump and the fall (the stick is only
' read while standing, $0936); falls a cell a pass unless standing on
' something or holding a ladder ($0A96); climbs up while his body is on a
' ladder or a gem or his legs on a ladder ($0B25), down when a ladder or gem
' is under his feet or his legs are on a ladder over empty space ($0B53);
' all the tests of a pass use the cells where the pass began; a gem is taken
' when his body or legs touch it, 100 ($0A1D); down + button takes or puts
' a block at the cell below-ahead, up + button (alone) takes or puts a
' ladder at his legs, four of each at most, and a failed take/put with the
' button just pressed is a jump ($0962-$0A1A); bombs: one per round,
' starting at the four places of $1161/$1169, speed = the screen's value -
' round / 2 pixels a pass on each axis, heading for a target, turning
' towards it every STEP passes, and every RETARGET turns they stop for nine
' passes with the fuse burning and take the miner's position as the new
' target ($0BD0-$0CBA); first turn counters from the clock (1, 3, 5 or 7);
' bombs wait 10 passes at a screen start ($115D); goblet 200, the bombs stand
' still 81 passes ($0CC2), it comes back after a death; touching a bomb
' loses a life, the screen starts again as new (blocks, ladders, gems)
' with the score kept; 4 lives in hand at the start, one more for each
' screen cleared (10 at most, $0DEB); after round 4 YOU WIN ($0E20).
'
' Liberties: the C64's cavern is 39 x 23 cells, here 28 x 20: the screens
' are fitted by scaling each run's end points (levels.py), a gem landing on
' a block moved up a row (the gems that need tools are the same ones as on
' the C64, orig/reach64.mjs and reach.mjs, but fewer cells to cross make
' some screens a little easier); bomb starts and the goblet placed by the
' same scaling; bomb speeds times 3/4 (rounded) to suit the smaller cavern
' (the C64's 8 pixels a pass at round 1, screen 1, is 6 here); one pass
' every 6, 5, 4 or 3 frames for speeds 1-4 (the C64's delay loop
' gave about 13 to 22 passes a second; speed 1 here is slower than the
' C64's slowest), sprites glide between passes; hits are box tests at the
' end of a pass (miner 6 x 15 pixels, bomb 12 x 13; the C64 used sprite
' collisions); digging uses the last way the miner walked (the C64 also
' dug to the left while he climbed); speed chosen on the title with the
' stick (the C64 used keys 1-4); the R (skip screen) and Q (quit) keys and
' the SHIFT pause are left out; a ROUND / SCREEN card before each screen,
' the miner blinks before a screen starts again; gems twinkle; bombs turn
' grey while the goblet holds them (the C64 flashed the top line); GAME
' OVER or YOU WIN, then the title (the C64 waited for the button).
' Eased for human players (playability pass): bombs wait 25 passes at a
' screen start (the C64's 10); after a lost life the miner blinks and
' bombs can't hurt him for 24 passes (the goblet still works); at speed 1
' each round has one bomb fewer (1, 1, 2, 3).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

